Keep a Linux web view's cookies on disk when its owner names a user-data folder.

JUCE never gives WebKitGTK's cookie manager a persistent store (its own
clearCookies() says the Linux backend "does not store cookies"), so the jar
lives in memory. Every WebBrowserComponent also runs in a forked child process
of its own, so the jar dies with the window: each new browser starts signed out
of every site. Plectrify's TONE3000 window is rebuilt for every fresh OAuth
authorization, which meant signing in to TONE3000 again for every tone picked.

Options has no Linux storage location, but WinWebView2 already carries a
per-browser user-data folder for exactly this purpose, so the Linux backend now
reads it too: when it is set, the child points the view's cookie manager at
<folder>/cookies.sqlite before the first load. A browser that names no folder
keeps JUCE's in-memory behaviour unchanged. Each child has its own default
context, so the store reaches that view alone.

